Python was developed by Guido van Rossum and released in 1991
It is older than Java, which was released in 1995
Earlier, it wasn't a popular language, but in recent years, Python is second most popular programming Language. 
Reason is its simplicity. For any Programming Language, having simple syntax is the most desirable feature, and Python best fits in it. 
As a programmer, you don't need to spend more time learning the Programming Language; you want to spend time building logic and implementing the software.
For example, the first program we write in Java, in C, or C++ to print Hello is lengthy. But in python its simply only 1 line Print("Hello").
Python is a simple yet very powerful language. It is a general-purpose programming language; you can build any kind of application. 
For Example: Console applications, Desktop applications, web applications, Gaming applications, AI, ML, IOT, and almost every kind of application.
Python can connect to database systems. It can also read and modify files in the database system.
Python can be used to handle big data and perform complex mathematics.
Python can be used for rapid prototyping or for production-ready software development.

Now Why Python?
* Python operates across multiple operating systems, including Windows, macOS, Linux, and single-board computers like Raspberry Pi.
* The language features an approachable syntax closely resembling standard English.
* It enables developers to implement programs using fewer lines of code compared to several alternative programming environments.
* Operating on an interpreter-based model, Python executes code immediately upon writing, which significantly accelerates the prototyping phase.
* Developers can approach coding in Python through procedural, object-oriented, or functional paradigms.
* The architecture prioritizes visual readability by blending English-like structures with mathematical conventions.
Syntax Comparison:
* Unlike many programming languages that rely on semicolons or parentheses to terminate commands, Python utilizes standard line breaks.
* Rather than employing curly brackets, Python defines code blocks and scope for loops, functions, and classes through consistent whitespace indentation.
